WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:01.254
Let's talk about Python.

2
00:00:01.775 --> 00:00:03.539
It's a language, but not the kind we

3
00:00:03.579 --> 00:00:04.421
speak every day.

4
00:00:04.461 --> 00:00:06.867
Instead, it's a way to communicate with

5
00:00:06.927 --> 00:00:07.428
computers.

6
00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:09.954
You see, computers can't guess what we

7
00:00:09.994 --> 00:00:10.194
mean.

8
00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:11.998
They need clear instructions.

9
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.005
A programming language like Python gives

10
00:00:15.065 --> 00:00:16.669
us a precise way to write those

11
00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:17.972
instructions.

12
00:00:17.972 --> 00:00:20.535
Python is popular because it reads almost

13
00:00:20.595 --> 00:00:22.657
like English, making it easier for

14
00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:23.198
beginners.

15
00:00:23.738 --> 00:00:26.061
It was created by Guido van Rossum and

16
00:00:26.141 --> 00:00:27.643
first released in 1991.

17
00:00:27.743 --> 00:00:31.707
Interestingly, it’s named after Monty

18
00:00:31.747 --> 00:00:33.930
Python, not the snake.

19
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.372
Now, let's look at how Python translates

20
00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:37.894
our instructions into actions.

21
00:00:38.414 --> 00:00:41.752
[DIAGRAM: python-translator] So, why do

22
00:00:41.772 --> 00:00:43.334
many beginners start with Python?

23
00:00:43.874 --> 00:00:45.817
First, it has very little ceremony.

24
00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:48.320
You can write a single, readable line of

25
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.662
code that does real work.

26
00:00:50.162 --> 00:00:52.385
Plus, there's a huge community out there.

27
00:00:52.906 --> 00:00:54.588
Almost any question you might have has an

28
00:00:54.728 --> 00:00:55.489
answer online.

29
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.531
And what’s great is that the same

30
00:00:57.612 --> 00:00:59.894
language can scale from tiny scripts to

31
00:00:59.954 --> 00:01:01.476
serious software.

32
00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:03.619
It's flexible and powerful.

33
00:01:04.021 --> 00:01:06.625
Python is used in many exciting areas.

34
00:01:07.185 --> 00:01:09.749
For example, it powers websites and apps

35
00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:11.511
like Instagram and Spotify.

36
00:01:12.192 --> 00:01:14.195
It's also used for data analysis and

37
00:01:14.255 --> 00:01:16.398
science, making it like spreadsheets on

38
00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:16.999
steroids.

39
00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:19.983
Python can automate boring tasks, like

40
00:01:20.043 --> 00:01:22.045
renaming a thousand files in just a

41
00:01:22.085 --> 00:01:22.446
second.

42
00:01:23.007 --> 00:01:24.809
And when it comes to AI and machine

43
00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:26.752
learning, most of that is written in

44
00:01:26.812 --> 00:01:27.733
Python.

45
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:30.196
Now, let’s see a visual of where Python

46
00:01:30.276 --> 00:01:30.716
is used.

47
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:33.299
[DIAGRAM: where-python-runs]

48
00:01:34.421 --> 00:01:35.322
Ready to get started?

49
00:01:35.882 --> 00:01:38.045
First, you'll need to install Python.

50
